\section{Top-level structure}{

A config document is a mapping with an optional \code{version} (defaults to
\code{1}; no other value is currently supported), an optional
\code{disable_existing_loggers} flag, and the optional sections \code{layouts},
\code{filters}, \code{appenders} and \code{loggers}. \code{loggers} is a mapping from logger
name (\code{root} for the root logger, \code{/}-separated names such as \code{app/db} for
descendants) to a logger definition supporting \code{class} (\code{"Logger"} or
\code{"LoggerGlue"}), \code{threshold}, \code{appenders}, \code{filters}, \code{propagate} and
\code{exception_handler}. \code{layouts}/\code{filters}/\code{appenders} are mappings from a
user-chosen component name to a component definition: a mapping with a
\code{class} key (e.g. \code{class: AppenderConsole}, optionally namespaced as
\code{pkg::ClassName}) plus the remaining keys as constructor arguments.
Component names can be referenced by name wherever a \code{layouts}/\code{filters}/
\code{appenders} value is expected (in another component, or in a logger); such
references are resolved once and the resulting object is shared. Appenders
and filters can also be defined inline instead of referenced by name.

Function-valued positions (a logger's \code{exception_handler}, and filter
entries) accept one of: a \strong{name reference} (a bare \code{character} scalar
for \code{exception_handler}, or an explicit \verb{\{ ref: <name> \}}), resolved by
lookup (\verb{<pkg>::<name>} supported); an \strong{inline code} form
(\code{{ code: "<R source>" }}), the only form that evaluates config-supplied
source and is therefore rejected unless the loader is called with
\code{allow_code = TRUE}; and, for filters only, a \verb{\{ class: <FilterClass>, ... \}} spec (constructing an R6 \link{EventFilter}) or a bare-string reference
to a named \code{filters} component.
}

\section{Environment variable interpolation}{

Scalar string values may contain \verb{$\{VAR\}} (substituted with the value of
environment variable \code{VAR}) or \verb{$\{VAR:-default\}} (using \code{default} when
\code{VAR} is unset or empty). A literal \verb{$\{...\}} can be produced with the
escape \verb{$$\{...\}}. A value produced \emph{entirely} by interpolation is
leniently coerced (number-like to \code{numeric}, \code{true}/\code{false} to \code{logical}),
so \verb{size: $\{SIZE\}} behaves like a literal \code{size: 1048576}. Interpolation
happens before validation and is never applied to \verb{code:} payloads.
}

\section{Safety}{

Loading a config never evaluates config-supplied R source by default.
Only the \verb{code:} form does so, and it is rejected during validation
unless the caller passes \code{allow_code = TRUE}. All other forms (name/\code{ref}
references, \code{class} specs, named-component references) are data, not
source, and are always available.
}

